The Rust binding links the installed pancake archives directly — the core and
blocking transports statically, the iroh companion as a shared library — and
mirrors the à-la-carte archive set with Cargo features. The crate root always
links `libpancake_ocapn` and presents a typed API in
[`bindings/rust/src/lib.rs`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/rust/src/lib.rs).
Each transport is its own feature-gated module — `tcp` and `inproc` on by
default, `iroh` opt-in — so a build links exactly the archives its features turn
on. The TCP `Netlayer`/`Listener`/`Endpoint` live under `pancake::tcp`. You work
with a `Peer` and Rust enums for `Reference`, `Argument`, and `Event`, so the
wire's values arrive as ordinary Rust you can match on.

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip — connect, fetch, call,
handle the reply — reads like this in Rust. The snippet uses the crate's opt-in
`async` feature, covered below, and runs inside a `tokio::task::LocalSet`.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```rust
> use pancake::{connect, tcp_locator, Argument};
>
> // tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> let partner = tcp_locator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> let conn = connect(&partner, None).await?;
> let greeter = conn.fetch("greeter").await?; // a capability, by name
> match conn.send(&greeter, "greet", &[Argument::String("world".into())]).await {
>     Ok(Argument::String(reply)) => println!("{reply}"),
>     Ok(other) => println!("unexpected reply: {other:?}"),
>     Err(reason) => eprintln!("the greeter refused: {reason:?}"),
> }
> conn.close();
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```rust
> // Cargo.toml: pancake = { …, features = ["iroh"] }
> use pancake::{connect, Argument, Reachability};
>
> // iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. Discovery resolves
> // the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> let partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh";
>
> let conn = connect(partner, Some(Reachability::Discovery)).await?;
> let greeter = conn.fetch("greeter").await?; // a capability, by name
> match conn.send(&greeter, "greet", &[Argument::String("world".into())]).await {
>     Ok(Argument::String(reply)) => println!("{reply}"),
>     Ok(other) => println!("unexpected reply: {other:?}"),
>     Err(reason) => eprintln!("the greeter refused: {reason:?}"),
> }
> conn.close();
> ```

The iroh tab swaps the built host-and-port locator for a bare node id, puts a
posture in place of `None`, and needs the `iroh` Cargo feature; everything
below `connect` is the tcp tab, line for line. A build without the feature refuses an iroh locator with a rebuild hint. The node id itself still arrives out of band, exactly like a
host and port; discovery turns a name you were given into a route, it does
not browse for peers.

## Async

The async surface is [tokio](https://tokio.rs/), behind an opt-in `async`
feature. A `Connection` runs the sans-io peer over a `tokio::net::TcpStream`.
Because the peer is a single-owner state machine, the `Connection` is `!Send`:
you create and use it on one task inside a `LocalSet`, where the peer calls stay
synchronous and only the socket reads and writes await. A `tell`, the
fire-and-forget send, returns once the deliver is queued on that driver task,
and the task flushes it.

## Actors

The actor layer is an `actors::Vat` over `FnMut(&str, &[Argument])` closures. A
local actor is a Rust closure, not a foreign callback, and a leading symbol
argument is the method verb. An eventual-send to a partner-hosted reference
settles with `conn.send(...).await` on the async surface; on the sans-io
`actors::Vat` itself, `Vat::send` returns a `Vow` — a local vow settles as the
vat drives its turns, a far one when the partner's resolve is dispatched
through `Vat::serve`.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build rust-dogfood    # cargo test against the pancake archives, sync + async
```

The full surface lives in the
[Rust binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/rust/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/rust/client.rs)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
